    {
      "thumb": "https://cdn.bsky.app/img/feed_thumbnail/plain/did:plc:4gm5uxjg3tz4nat4qpjk2t6h/bafkreibglcrpx3zyhbawrg2ihtse7ypkarqahsmliqesuggtgqusoi7hca",
      "fullsize": "https://cdn.bsky.app/img/feed_fullsize/plain/did:plc:4gm5uxjg3tz4nat4qpjk2t6h/bafkreibglcrpx3zyhbawrg2ihtse7ypkarqahsmliqesuggtgqusoi7hca",
      "alt": "The career claim. It's consistent across a decade of interviews. He joined the South African police in 1990, and in 1992 a photography unit was formed within the Riot Unit to document political violence in the townships, partly because foreign photographers were too scared to go in and police were getting danger pay. He's said he volunteered for the car, not the craft: he had no photographic inclination and ended up the photographer by default. He left the force in 1997 and moved into estate-agent headshots, then lingerie and surfwear catalogs, then luxury weddings. I found no independent SAP records, but the story is stable and detailed enough that fabrication seems unlikely.  (Interview With Wedding and Fashion Photographer Brett Florens | Tiltshots +2)\n\nThe bigger flag is the framing. His marketing bio says a photographic unit was formed to document political changes and he jumped at the chance. What a white conscript in a KwaZulu-Natal riot unit was actually doing in 1990–97 is documenting the ANC–IFP conflict from inside the apartheid police, an institution the TRC later found had armed and abetted one side of that violence. He calls it \"the most dangerous place on earth\" and \"national service obligations\" without ever naming who the danger was to or what the police's role in it was. That's not a lie; it's a very well-worn omission.",
      "ratio": 0.7148,
      "handle": "hotrollhottakes.bsky.social",
      "url": "https://bsky.app/profile/hotrollhottakes.bsky.social/post/3mvyb2pgouk25",
      "uri": "at://did:plc:4gm5uxjg3tz4nat4qpjk2t6h/app.bsky.feed.post/3mvyb2pgouk25",
      "likeCount": 11
    },
    {
      "thumb": "https://cdn.bsky.app/img/feed_thumbnail/plain/did:plc:4gm5uxjg3tz4nat4qpjk2t6h/bafkreihulib327px5tsynqxm7xfraw3hdfcypcerbuzj5cb4bvkyejf374",
      "fullsize": "https://cdn.bsky.app/img/feed_fullsize/plain/did:plc:4gm5uxjg3tz4nat4qpjk2t6h/bafkreihulib327px5tsynqxm7xfraw3hdfcypcerbuzj5cb4bvkyejf374",
      "alt": "Two smaller things worth knowing:\nBy his own account, while still a cop, he realized he could make extra cash by selling his crime-scene images and video to agencies and media himself, so he started buying his own film. Selling evidence photos from active township murders for personal profit is an ethics problem he narrates as hustle.  (SmugMug)\n\nOn clients, he says he uses neurolinguistic programming and body-language techniques on people in emotionally vulnerable situations to get them to trust him quickly. That's a manipulation toolkit described with pride, and it's the through-line from the riot unit to the wedding: proximity to people on their worst or most exposed day, monetized.  (Tiltshots)",
